In section III, we introduce the minimal \(t\)-\(V_{1}\)-\(V_{2}\) effective model. To capture the basic physics of the leading instability at the vH filling and reveal the nature of the competitive bond CDW states, we project the inter-site \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\) interaction terms onto the vH states. This puts the physical states of the electrons at the \(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha}\) points. We find an exact result that the projected inter-sublattice Coulomb interactions \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\) couple precisely the antisymmetry bond, corresponding to the nn and nnn breathing modes [48], respectively. The weak-coupling instability at vH filling is thus completely determined by the aforementioned bond susceptibilities, which allow us to show that while \(V_{1}\) drives a real bond CDW order, \(V_{2}\) drives a competing imaginary bond CDW order corresponding to a LC Chern insulator.

In section IV, we perform a self-consistent mean-field calculation for the \(t\)-\(V_{1}\)-\(V_{2}\) model and obtain the phase diagram that reveals the orbital Chern insulator phases of different LC patterns, separated from the topological trivial ISD phase by a first order phase transition. We further demonstrate that electron doping away from vH filling helps the stabilization of the LC states, giving rise to the conjectured LC Chern metal with CFPs carrying large Berry curvature and orbital magnetic moment [48]. The summary and discussions are provided in section V in connection to the experimental evidence for the TRS breaking charge ordered states in the kagome superconductors.

## II Tight-binding model and bond susceptibilities.

The single-orbital tight-binding model with nn hopping \(t\) on the kagome lattice is depicted in Fig. 1a. The locations of the three sublattices in the unit cell at \(\mathbf{r}\) are given by \(\mathbf{r}_{1}=\mathbf{r}-\frac{1}{2}\alpha_{3}\), \(\mathbf{r}_{2}=\mathbf{r}\), and \(\mathbf{r}_{3}=\mathbf{r}+\frac{1}{2}\alpha_{1}\), with the lattice constant \(a_{0}\equiv 1\). Along each \(\mathbf{a}_{\alpha}\) direction, there are two independent nn bonds within a unit cell, which define the symmetric (\(+\)) and antisymmetric (\(-\)) bond operators [48],

\[\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm}(\mathbf{r})=c^{\dagger}_{\beta\mathbf{r }}c_{\gamma\mathbf{\tau}}\pm c^{\dagger}_{\beta\mathbf{r}}c_{\gamma\mathbf{\tau}-\mathbf{ a}_{\gamma}}, \tag{1}\]

where \(c^{\dagger}_{\alpha\mathbf{\tau}}\) creates an electron on sublattice \(\alpha\) in unit cell \(\mathbf{r}\). The sublattice indices run over \((\alpha,\beta,\gamma)=(1,\,2,\,3)\), \((2,\,3,\,1)\), \((3,\,1,\,2)\) and spin indices are left implicit. The unique sublattice geometry of the kagome lattice is clearly revealed by rewriting the tight-binding model as

\[H_{\text{fb}}=-t\sum_{\alpha,\pm}\left[\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm} (\mathbf{r})+h.c.\right]-\mu\sum_{\alpha,\pm}c^{\dagger}_{\alpha\mathbf{\tau}}c_{ \alpha\mathbf{\tau}}, \tag{2}\]

where \(\mu\) is the chemical potential. To study the instabilities toward the real and imaginary CDW in the in-phase and the out-of-phase (breathing) bond channels separately, we divide the symmetric and antisymmetric bonds into their real and imaginary components,

\[\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm}=\frac{1}{2}\left[\hat{\gamma}^{\text{ nn}}_{\alpha,\pm}+(\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm})^{\dagger}\right],\quad \hat{\gamma}^{\prime\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm}=\frac{1}{2i}\left[\hat{\gamma}^{ \text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm}-(\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm})^{\dagger}\right], \tag{3}\]

and compute the corresponding bare susceptibilities for the tight-binding noninteracting electrons

\[\Pi_{\hat{O}}(\mathbf{q})=\lim_{\omega=0}i\int_{0}^{\infty}dte^{i\omega t} \left\langle\left[\hat{O}(\mathbf{q},t),\hat{O}^{\dagger}(-\mathbf{q},0) \right]\right\rangle, \tag{4}\]

with \(\hat{O}\) being one of the four bond operators in each \(\mathbf{a}_{\alpha}\) direction, \(\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm}\) and \(\hat{\gamma}^{\prime\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm}\). A temperature of \(k_{B}T=0.005t\) is applied in the calculation to avoid the logarithmic divergences tied to the vH singularities.

At band filling \(n_{\text{vH}}=5/12\), the sublattice-resolved FS is the hexagon connecting the vH singularities at the \(\mathbf{M}\) points of the BZ, as shown in Fig. 1b. The three vH points are connected to each other by the \(2\times 2\) wavevectors \(\mathbf{Q}_{\alpha}=\mathbf{M}_{\alpha}\). Since the Bloch states at these three vH points are localized on different sublattices, the instability towards \(2\times 2\) onsite charge density order on the same sublattice is significantly suppressed, and the most likely \(2\times 2\) order is off-site bond order that connect two sites belonging to two different sublattices. The calculated bare susceptibilities of the four nn bonds in the \(\alpha=1\) direction, i.e., \(\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{1,\pm}\) and \(\hat{\gamma}^{\prime\text{nn}}_{1,\pm}\), are plotted along the high-symmetry path in the BZ and compared directly in Fig. 1c. The susceptibilities in the \(\alpha\neq 1\) directions can be obtained from \(\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{1,\pm}\) and \(\hat{\gamma}^{\prime\text{nn}}_{1,\pm}\) by \(C_{3}\) rotations. It is clear that the susceptibilities of the symmetric nn bonds are much weaker than those of the antisymmetric nn bonds in the breathing channel with the divergence reflected by the peaks located at \(\mathbf{Q}_{1}\), the wavevector connecting the two vH points containing the sublattice \(2\) and \(3\), as shown in Fig. 1b. The leading divergence is in the susceptibility of the real antisymmetric bonds, pointing to leading instability toward a \(2\times 2\) real CDW driven by nn interaction \(V_{1}\), consistent with previous investigations [52; 60].

The susceptibilities of the nnn bonds are intriguing. Just as for the nn bonds, there are two independent nnn bonds in a unit cell along the direction _perpendicular_ to each basis vector \(\mathbf{a}_{\alpha}\) (i.e., along \(\mathbf{Q}_{\alpha}\) direction), connecting two sites of different sublattices, as shown in Fig. 1a. Define symmetric (\(+\)) and antisymmetric (\(-\)) nnn bonds, and divide them into real and imaginary components,

\[\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nnnn}}_{\alpha,\pm}(\mathbf{r})=c^{\dagger}_{\beta\mathbf{ \kappa}}c_{\gamma\mathbf{\tau}+\mathbf{a}_{\mu}}\pm c^{\dagger}_{\beta\mathbf{\kappa}}c_{ \gamma\mathbf{\tau}+\mathbf{a}_{\gamma}}=\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nnnn}}_{\alpha,\pm}( \mathbf{r})+\hat{\gamma}^{\prime\text{nnnn}}_{\alpha,\pm}(\mathbf{r}). \tag{5}\]

The calculated bare susceptibilities of the four nnn bonds in the \(\alpha=1\) direction, i.e., \(\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nnn}}_{1,\pm}\) and \(\hat{\gamma}^{\prime\text{nnn}}_{1,\pm}\), are plotted in Fig. 1d. In contrast to the nn bonds, the imaginary nnn bond in the antisymmetric breathing channel \(\hat{\gamma}^{\prime\text{nnnn}}_{1,\pm}\) has now the leading divergent susceptibility, while the real counterpart \(\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nnn}}_{1,\pm}\) is subleading. As a result, we expect the nnn Coulomb interaction \(V_{2}\) on kagome lattice to play a crucial role in driving an instability toward the spontaneous TRS breaking \(2\times 2\) CDW with LC order. This motivates us to study the concrete \(t\)-\(V_{1}\)-\(V_{2}\) model.

## III Interatomic interactions and projection to van Hove modes

### The \(t\)-\(V_{1}\)-\(V_{2}\) model

The single-orbital \(t\)-\(V_{1}\)-\(V_{2}\) model is given by

\[H=-t\sum_{\langle i,j\rangle,\sigma}(c^{\dagger}_{i\sigma}c_{j \sigma}+h.c.)+V_{1}\sum_{\langle i,j\rangle}\hat{n}_{i}\hat{n}_{j}+V_{2}\sum_{ \langle i,j\rangle}\hat{n}_{i}\hat{n}_{j}, \tag{6}\]

where \(c^{\dagger}_{i\sigma}\) creates a spin-\(\sigma\) electron on site \(i\), and the density operator \(\hat{n}_{i}=\sum_{\sigma}c^{\dagger}_{i\sigma}c_{i\sigma}\). Hereafter, we set the nn hopping \(t\equiv 1\) to be the energy unit. To bring out the sublattice structures on the kagome lattice, we write

\[H=H_{\text{fb}}+H_{V_{1}}+H_{V_{2}}, \tag{7}\]

where \(H_{\text{fb}}\) is the tight-binding part given in Eq. (2) and

\[H_{V_{1}}= V_{1}\sum_{\alpha,\mathbf{r}}(\hat{n}_{\beta\alpha}\hat{n}_{ \gamma\mathbf{r}}+\hat{n}_{\beta\alpha}\hat{n}_{\gamma\mathbf{r}-\alpha_{ \alpha}}) \tag{8}\] \[H_{V_{2}}= V_{2}\sum_{\alpha,\mathbf{r}}(\hat{n}_{\beta\alpha}\hat{n}_{ \gamma\mathbf{r}+\alpha_{\beta}}+\hat{n}_{\beta\alpha}\hat{n}_{\gamma\mathbf{ r}+\alpha_{\gamma}}). \tag{9}\]

Expanding the electron operator on the \(\alpha\) sublattice located in the \(\mathbf{r}\) unit cell in terms of the complete set of momenta in the first BZ,

\[c_{\alpha\mathbf{r}}=\sum_{\mathbf{k}\in 1BZ}e^{-i\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{r}}c_{ \alpha\mathbf{k}},\quad c^{\dagger}_{\alpha\mathbf{r}}=\sum_{\mathbf{k}\in 1 BZ}e^{i\mathbf{k}\cdot\mathbf{r}}c^{\dagger}_{\alpha\mathbf{k}}, \tag{10}\]

we obtain the Fourier transform of the inter-site interactions,

\[H_{V_{1,2}}=-\sum_{\alpha\mathbf{q}}\sum_{\mathbf{k}\mathbf{k}^ {\prime}}V^{\alpha}_{1,2}(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime},\mathbf{q})c^{ \dagger}_{\beta\mathbf{k}}c_{\gamma\mathbf{k}+\alpha}c^{\dagger}_{\gamma \mathbf{k}^{\prime}}c_{\beta\mathbf{k}^{\prime}-\mathbf{q}}, \tag{11}\]

where the corresponding form factors for the nn \(V_{1}\) and nnn \(V_{2}\) Coulomb repulsions are given by

\[V^{\alpha}_{1}(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime},\mathbf{q})= V_{1}[1+e^{i(\mathbf{k}-\mathbf{k}^{\prime}+\mathbf{q})\cdot\alpha_{ \alpha}}], \tag{12}\] \[V^{\alpha}_{2}(\mathbf{k},\mathbf{k}^{\prime},\mathbf{q})= V_{2}[e^{-i(\mathbf{k}-\mathbf{k}^{\prime}+\mathbf{q})\cdot\alpha_{ \beta}}+e^{-i(\mathbf{k}-\mathbf{k}^{\prime}+\mathbf{q})\cdot\alpha_{\gamma}}]. \tag{13}\]

### Projected interactions and van Hove modes

At vH filling, the tight-binding FS is the hexagon with corners located at the vH points \(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha}\) where the density of states diverges (Fig. 1b). To study the leading instability of the electronic states, we project the physical states onto these vH modes under the projection operator \(\hat{P}=\sum_{\alpha}|\mathbf{M}_{\alpha}\rangle(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha}|\). Since these states are localized on independent sublattices, the projection is accomplished by putting the momenta of the electron operators in Eq. (11) on the vH points of the corresponding sublattices, \(\mathbf{k}=\mathbf{k}^{\prime}-\mathbf{q}=\mathbf{M}_{\beta}\) and \(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{q}=\mathbf{k}^{\prime}=\mathbf{M}_{\gamma}\), which amounts to \(\mathbf{k}=\mathbf{M}_{\beta}\), \(\mathbf{k}^{\prime}=\mathbf{M}_{\gamma}\), and \(\mathbf{q}=\mathbf{M}_{\alpha}\). To evaluate the form factors in Eqs (12-13), we note that \(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha}\cdot\mathbf{a}_{\beta}=\frac{1}{2}\mathbf{G}_{\alpha}\cdot \mathbf{a}_{\beta}=\pi(1-\delta_{\alpha\beta})\) (mod \(2\pi\)). Thus, the projected interactions read

\[H^{\text{vH}}_{V_{1,2}}=-2V_{1,2}\sum_{\alpha}c^{\dagger}_{\beta \mathbf{M}_{\alpha}}c_{\gamma\mathbf{M}_{\gamma}}c_{\beta\mathbf{M}_{\gamma}}. \tag{14}\]

Clearly, the extended Coulomb repulsions \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\) couple directly to the vH modes in a similar form.

It is instructive to write the projected interactions in terms of the bond operators. Since the nn and nnn bond operators in momentum space are given by

\[\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm}(\mathbf{q}) =\sum_{\mathbf{k}}\left[1\pm e^{i(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{q})\cdot \mathbf{\alpha}_{\alpha}}\right]c^{\dagger}_{\beta\mathbf{k}}c_{\gamma\mathbf{k}+ \mathbf{q}}, \tag{15}\] \[\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,\pm}(\mathbf{q}) =\sum_{\mathbf{k}}\left[e^{-i(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{q})\cdot\mathbf{ \alpha}_{\beta}}\pm e^{-i(\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{q})\cdot\mathbf{\alpha}_{\gamma}} \right]c^{\dagger}_{\beta\mathbf{k}}c_{\gamma\mathbf{k}+\mathbf{q}}, \tag{16}\]

projecting the fermions onto the vH modes yields

\[\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,+}(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha})=\hat{ \gamma}^{\text{mm}}_{\alpha,+}(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha})=0, \tag{17}\] \[\hat{\gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,-}(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha})=-\hat{ \gamma}^{\text{nm}}_{\alpha,-}(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha})=2c^{\dagger}_{\beta\mathbf{M }_{\gamma}}c_{\gamma\mathbf{M}_{\gamma}}. \tag{18}\]

Consequently, the intersite interactions in the projected vH manifold in Eq. (14) become

\[H^{\text{vH}}_{V_{1}}=-\frac{V_{1}}{2}\sum_{\alpha}\left|\hat{ \gamma}^{\text{nn}}_{\alpha,-}(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha})\right|^{2},\quad H^{\text{ vH}}_{V_{2}}=-\frac{V_{2}}{2}\sum_{\alpha}\left|\hat{\gamma}^{\text{mm}}_{ \alpha,-}(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha})\right|^{2}.\]

These results demonstrate explicitly that the extended Coulomb interactions drive the breathing instability of the kagome lattice represented by the antisymmetric bonds [48]. Combined with the bare susceptibilities calculated for the antisymmetry bonds shown in Fig. 1c-d, it is clear that there is a weak-coupling instability toward a real CDW due to nn \(V_{1}\), and one toward an imaginary CDW with LC due to nnn \(V_{2}\), respectively. When both \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\) are present, we find that the Landau free energy analysis [39] predicts a transition in the unstable vH modes toward an imaginary CDW with spontaneous TRS breaking when

\[\frac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}>\frac{\Pi^{\text{mm}}_{\alpha,-}(\mathbf{M }_{\alpha})-\Pi^{\prime\text{mn}}_{\alpha,-}(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha})}{\Pi^{ \prime\text{mm}}_{\alpha,-}(\mathbf{M}_{\alpha})-\Pi^{\text{nnn}}_{\alpha,-}( \mathbf{M}_{\alpha})}. \tag{19}\]

The value of this critical ratio can be estimated using the corresponding susceptibilities of the nn and nnn antisymmetric bonds in Fig. 1c-d, leading to \(V_{2}/V_{1}>(1.47-0.96)/(0.99-0.77)\simeq 2.36\).

## IV Mean-field theory and phase diagram

### Mean-field theory

Next, we go beyond the weak-coupling analysis and study the phase structure of the \(t\)-\(V_{1}\)-\(V_{2}\) model in Eq. (6) as a function of the correlations \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\) in a self-consistent mean-field theory. Decoupling the Coulomb repulsions \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\) in terms of the nn and nnn bonds, \(\hat{\gamma}_{ij}=c^{\dagger}_{i\uparrow}c_{j\uparrow}+c^{\dagger}_{i\downarrow} c_{j\downarrow}\), we obtained the mean-field Hamiltonian in real space,

\[H_{\text{MF}}= -\sum_{\langle i,j\rangle}\left[(t+V_{1}\chi^{*}_{ij})\hat{ \gamma}_{ij}+h.c.-V_{1}|\chi_{ij}|^{2}\right]\] \[-\sum_{\langle i,j\rangle}\left(V_{2}\chi^{*}_{ij}\hat{\gamma}_{ij} +h.c.-V_{2}|\chi_{ij}|^{2}\right). \tag{20}\]The order parameters of the nn and nnn bonds \(\chi_{ij}=\langle\hat{q}_{ij}\rangle\) are to be determined self-consistently.

A few remarks on the mean-field theory are in order. First of all, we consider the \(t\)-\(V_{1}\)-\(V_{2}\) model as an effective model for capturing the correlation effects beyond the DFT calculations, and as such, the direct Hatree terms from \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\) depending on the total density \(n_{i}=\langle\hat{n}_{i}\rangle\) are neglected to avoid double-counting [65], similar to the LDA+\(U\)+\(V\) approach [66; 67]. Moreover, note that the real uniform components of the bond order parameters \(\chi_{ij}\) in Eq. (20) induced by the Coulomb repulsions retain all the symmetries under the point group \(D_{6}\) of the kagome lattice. They merely introduce corrections to the nn and nnn hopping integrals that renormalize the bandwidth and modify or even remove the vH singularity at the \(\mathbf{M}\) points. Since the vH singularity at the \(\mathbf{M}\) points have been established by ARPES in all \(A\)V\({}_{3}\)Sb\({}_{5}\), we adapt a procedure where all the symmetry invariant corrections to the real uniform order parameter are subtracted in the interactions, such that the vH points and the band structure are maintained without adjusting the bare band parameters. Similar subtraction schemes have been used successfully in the Hartree-Fock mean-field calculations of the Coulomb interaction in twisted bilayer graphene [68; 69; 70; 71]. Implementing this subtraction scheme, the correlation effects in the self-consistent mean-field theory will only generate spontaneous symmetry-breaking states. We have verified that the subtraction only quantitatively shift the phase boundary in the phase diagram without changing our main conclusions on the emergence of the LC state.

### Bond order and LC patterns

To capture the physics in the vicinity of vH filling, we consider the quantum states described by the Hamiltonian in Eq. (20) to be periodic with an enlarged 2\(\times\)2 unit cell, enclosed by black dashed lines in Fig. 2. The enlarged unit cell contains 12 sites, 24 nn bonds, and 24 nnn bonds. A completely unbiased solution of the \(2\times 2\) superlattice thus involves determining the 48 independent bonds self-consistently. In this work, we focus on states with symmetries described by the point group \(C_{6}\), which is a subgroup of \(D_{6}\). This includes the LC states that break certain reflection symmetries spontaneously. The 6 sites on the inner/outter apexes of the green stars in Fig. 2a can be mapped onto each other by the point group symmetry operation of \(C_{6}\) and, consequently, belong to the same class of sites with identical properties. These two classes of sites are labeled as \(A\) and \(B\), respectively, and denoted by grey and black spheres in Fig. 2. For the nn and nnn bonds, we note that, in addition to the constraints imposed by \(C_{6}\) symmetry, currents associated with their imaginary parts must satisfy the continuity condition for charges to be conserved. As a result, the 24 nn bonds are reduced to three distinct classes as illustrated in Fig. 2a. Specifically, the 6 blue bonds forming the inner hexagon connect the \(A\) sites and are labeled by \(\chi_{1}\) in Fig. 2a; the 6 red bonds (\(\chi_{2}\)) of the outer two triangles link the \(B\) sites; and the 12 green bonds (\(\chi_{3}\)) of the star bridge the \(A\) and \(B\) sites. Similarly, the 24 nnn bonds are also reduced to three independent classes of equivalent ones, denoted by the colors of bonds and labeled as \(\chi^{\prime}_{1,2,3}\) in Fig. 2b. We note that, in these \(C_{6}\) symmetric states, the average density on the four nn sites and that on the four nnn sites of any lattice site equal to the average density of the system. As a result, the Hartree term, even if considered, would only introduce effectively a global chemical potential term to the Hamiltonian and thus physically irrelevant.

In Fig. 2, the anticlockwise winding arrows on each class of bonds signify the forward flowing direction of the currents on these bonds. They define the current direction associated with a positive Im(\(\chi_{1,2,3}\)) or Im(\(\chi^{\prime}_{1,2,3}\)). The physical current lives on the nn bonds and the current on the nnn bonds is associated with the quantum fluctuations of the charge density. The current direction on each class of bonds can be reversed without violating any constraint imposed by \(C_{6}\) symmetry or the continuity condition. Note that two states with all currents flowing in opposite directions can be mapped to each other by reflection, and share identical energy and quasiparticle band structure. Furthermore, as we shall show later, physical properties, topological ones in particular, are controlled by the current pattern on the nn bonds. Therefore, we fix the current direction on \(\chi_{1}\) bonds to be anticlockwise, and use the current pattern on the three classes of the nn bonds to define the LC states. Consequently, there are in total four possible LC states: LC\({}_{1}\) with currents flowing anticlockwise in all three classes of nn bonds (Fig. 2a), LC\({}_{2}\) with currents on \(\chi_{2}\) bonds flipped, LC\({}_{3}\) with currents on \(\chi_{3}\) bonds reversed, and LC\({}_{4}\) with currents on both \(\chi_{2}\) and \(\chi_{3}\) bonds over-turned. We will show that all these LC states appear on the phase diagram spanned by \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\) in the self-consistent mean-field theory.

In order to obtain all the possible states, we use different initial conditions for solving the self-consistency equations numerically. Besides the four LC states mentioned above, we also encounter converged solutions of two real CDW states, SD and ISD, where all bonds are real and the LC is absent. In these real CDW states, \(\chi_{1}\) and \(\chi_{2}\) are close in value, which is weaker (stronger) than \(\chi_{3}\) in the SD (ISD) configuration with the stronger bonds forming the stars (hexagon and triangles). Moreover, the LC states we found have complex bonds \(\chi_{1,2,3}\) and \(\chi^{\prime}_{1,2,3}\) and are thus complex bond CDW states. While the real part of these complex bonds may deviate from SD or

