ISD configurations, we will denote these states according to their current patterns as LC\({}_{1,-4}\), unless otherwise notes. When multiple converged states exist for a given set of parameters, \(V=(V_{1},V_{2})\), we compare the state energies to determine the true ground state.

### Phase diagram and transitions at vH filling

#### iii.3.1 Phase structure of ISD and LC states

We first study the zero-temperature phase structure and the formation of the LC states at vH filling with \(n=n_{\rm vH}=5/12\). The ground state phase diagram is presented in Fig. 3 as a function of nn \(V_{1}\) and nnn \(V_{2}\). In the absence of nnn Coulomb repulsion, \(V_{2}=0\), only two states can be self-consistently obtained, ISD and LC\({}_{2}\), with the ISD state lower in energy in the entire parameter regime we explored. Thus, LC\({}_{2}\) is a metastable state with higher energy in this case. In LC\({}_{2}\) at \(V_{2}=0\), the real parts of \(\chi_{1}\) and \(\chi_{2}\) are close in value and much larger than Re(\(\chi_{3}\)), forming a SD pattern. Switching on the nnn Coulomb repulsion \(V_{2}\), we find that various LC states can be stabilized as the ground state when \(V_{2}\) is sufficiently large and the ISD gives way to the LC states via a first-order transition. Overall, the currents on \(\chi_{3}\) bonds tend to flow anticlockwise at small \(V_{1}\) and clockwise at large \(V_{1}\), while the currents on \(\chi_{2}\) bonds prefer to flow clockwise at small \(V_{2}\) and anticlockwise at large \(V_{2}\), producing the rich phase diagram shown in Fig. 3, where LC\({}_{2}\), the LC state studied phenomenologically in Ref. [48], occupies the phase regime for small and moderate \(V_{1}\) and \(V_{2}\).

The phase diagram (Fig. 3) consists of five ground states, an ISD and four LC states. Their schematic illustration at representative values of \(V=(V_{1},V_{2})\) and the corresponding quasiparticle dispersions are displayed in Fig. 4, with the self-consistently determined nn and nnn bond expectation values listed in Table 1. Because of the instabilities at vH filling, ground states in the entire phase diagram, except for the non-interacting point at the origin \(V_{1,2}=0\), exhibit spontaneous symmetry breaking and are insulating states, which can be seen in the quasiparticle dispersions. The ISD state breaks only the lattice translation symmetry and is topologically trivial, while the LC states break TRS additionally and are topologically nontrivial on the kagome lattice.

#### iii.3.2 Topological properties of the LC states

Fig. 4 shows that each isolated quasiparticle band in LC states acquires an integer topological Chern number. It is important to note that, while the Chern number of each band may change its value as the interaction parameters (\(V_{1},V_{2}\)) change in a particular LC regime in the ground state phase diagram, the total Chern number of all occupied bands \(\mathcal{N}\) remains unchanged and therefore serves as the integer topological invariant of the LC state. Explicitly, we find \(\mathcal{N}=1\), \(-1\), \(0\), and \(-1\), respectively, for LC\({}_{1}\), LC\({}_{2}\), LC\({}_{3}\), and LC\({}_{4}\). The \(\mathcal{N}=\pm 2\) LC states predicted by statistical learning [47] do not appear in the mean-field phase diagram of the \(t\)-\(V_{1}\)-\(V_{2}\) model. Interestingly, LC\({}_{2}\) and LC\({}_{4}\) separated by dashed thin line in Fig. 3 have the same \(\mathcal{N}\) and will be shown below to be in the same phase, whereas all other boundaries between two states with different total Chern numbers \(\mathcal{N}\) are first-order phase transitions, as indicated by solid thick lines in the phase diagram (Fig. 3). The insulating LC states with nonzero total Chern numbers are thus orbital Chern insulators, since the TRS is broken by the orbital LC order [41; 42; 48]. Moreover, since the LC order has a nonzero momentum, the sum of the staggered flux around each irreducible plaquette in the \(2\times 2\) unit cell is strictly zero, similar to an antiferromagnetic (AF) ordered state. Therefore, the LC states can be viewed as orbital antiferromagnets [48]. The states LC\({}_{1,2,4}\) have nonzero \(\mathcal{N}\) and are therefore orbital AF Chern insulators, i.e. orbital analogs of the spin AF Chern insulators [72].

The current pattern on the nnn bonds in different LC states in the phase diagram is denoted by grey symbols "\(\pm\pm\pm\)" in Fig. 3. They refer to the directions of the currents on the nnn bonds, i.e. the sign of Im(\(\chi^{\prime}_{1,2,3}\)), with the"\(+\)" direction defined as in Fig. 2b. There exist two kinds of current patterns on the nnn bonds in the phases of LC\({}_{1,2,3}\), which are separated by dotted thin lines. We stress that the total Chern number \(\mathcal{N}\) does not change its value as the currents on nnn bonds change their patterns continuously across the dotted thin lines in Fig. 3, which implies \(\mathcal{N}\) is solely determined by the current pattern on the nn bonds, supporting our choice of using the latter alone to define these TRS broken states. However, as discussed below, the imaginary part of the nnn bonds can be

